<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<form name="frmWoD20_9" align="client" theme="dark">
	<script>
		<![CDATA[
			-- The three experience numbers are drawn by renderXPBoxes on the root form (SPEC
			-- I11): the Main tab shows the balance too, so the routine that writes it cannot
			-- live on either tab (SPEC R.9 / B.9). None of the three owns a `field=` any more -
			-- only the total behind them is saved, and Spent and the balance are worked out
			-- from it and the log (SPEC V100).

			-- The box no longer grows with the rows (SPEC I57, user 2026-08-23): it stands at
			-- the height the XML gives it and the COLUMNS grow inside a scrollBox of their own,
			-- so what runs past the frame is scrolled instead of pushing the frame down the tab.
			--
			-- The four columns share that one scrollBox rather than each carrying a bar, which
			-- is the whole reason the box used to grow: four bars scroll four different amounts
			-- and line N stops being line N across the row. One parent, one bar, one offset.
			--
			-- XP_LOG_MIN_H is the floor of the CONTENT now, not of the box - it keeps the empty
			-- log the same shape as a full one (SPEC V235).
			-- The height of the revert button, and NOTHING about rendered text (SPEC I167b, V486a,
			-- B166). It used to be declared as "the rendered line height of the five columns,
			-- MEASURED and not chosen" - it was neither measured nor the line height, and the log
			-- drifted for it. See XP_ROW_H below for what replaced that claim.
			local XP_LINE_H   = 16;

			-- A log row is TWICE the button, and BOTH numbers are ours (SPEC I167b, V486a, V486b).
			--
			-- These two used to claim to be a text metric: XP_LINE_H was "the rendered line height
			-- of the five columns, MEASURED and not chosen", and the rows were five text blocks
			-- joined with "\n\n" so a record occupied two of those lines. It was never measured.
			-- The screen says the pair is near 27 and the step said 32, so the X walked about 5px
			-- further from its record every row and by the thirteenth it pointed at a different
			-- purchase (SPEC B166). The rendered line height belongs to the HOST - this SDK
			-- reports no text metric at all (SPEC R165b) - so no integer here could ever have been
			-- right, and any authored step accumulates.
			--
			-- So the record stopped being text laid out by the host and became a control laid out
			-- by us: <layout name="xpRow_N" height="32"> with its five labels vertTextAlign=center.
			-- What these two constants name now is AUTHORED XML, measured on both sides by the
			-- gate (SPEC V486a, V486d):
			--
			--     XP_LINE_H == btnXpRev_N.height       XP_ROW_H == xpRow_N.height
			--     2 * btnXpRev_N.top-inside-its-row + XP_LINE_H == XP_ROW_H
			--
			-- and that second line is the whole of "the record's centre and the X's centre are one
			-- centre" (user 2026-09-08): both are the centre of the same layout, for row 1 and for
			-- row 60 by the same construction, with nothing left to drift.
			local XP_ROW_H    = 2 * XP_LINE_H;

			-- The floor the five columns never go under. They are GROUND now (SPEC I167c) - no
			-- text, just the grey band each column reads on, which is the control's own default
			-- shade and has no palette key that could reproduce it (SPEC R167a, V53) - but they
			-- are still what gives the scroll its reach, because gui.ScrollBox has no range
			-- property of its own and the children own it (SPEC R104, R167c).
			local XP_LOG_MIN_H = 470;

			-- What the storyteller's column of X buttons is worth in width (SPEC I163b, V478b).
			-- The XML authors the PLAYER - the narrow one - and this is what the log, its title,
			-- the APPLY box and its button all grow by when the reader turns out to be the
			-- storyteller. It is a RELATION and the gate measures it against the XML: the buttons
			-- close exactly this far past the last column.
			local XP_REV_W = 30;

			-- The two state sentences used to live IN the Type column, which was widened to 442 so
			-- it would cover its three empty neighbours. That is what made an empty log read as
			-- something broken instead of as an empty log (SPEC I39, V235, user 2026-08-22): the
			-- columns moved when there was nothing in them. The sentence has a label of its own
			-- now (dynXpEmpty) and no column ever changes width.

			-- ---- Experience log (SPEC I10, 27th round) ---------------------------------
			--
			-- Four columns, all four written from ONE row list, so a line reads across. They
			-- own no `field` (SPEC V84): the log is derived from the saved initial character
			-- and the sheet as it stands, exactly like the total above is derived from the two
			-- numbers beside it. A stored third copy could only drift.
			--
			-- The rows come from xpLedgerRows() on the root form (SPEC I9/I10); this routine
			-- only formats them.
			--
			-- `rows` is optional. A caller that has just built the list hands it over rather
			-- than making this walk the ledger a second time for the same click (SPEC V125) -
			-- that second walk is half of what made the sheet freeze (SPEC B34). Left out, the
			-- list is built here, and a nil coming back out of xpLedgerRows still means what it
			-- always did: no initial character has been saved yet.
			-- The five controls this routine writes. Found by name from whatever node it is
			-- handed (SPEC V143), so the same call works from this tab's own events and from
			-- the root, which is how a purchase made on ANY tab reaches the log without the
			-- tab having registered itself first (SPEC V133, B39).
			local XP_LOG = { dynXpType = true, dynXpTrait = true, dynXpLevel = true, dynXpCost = true, dynXpWhen = true, dynXpEmpty = true, xpApplyBox = true, btnXpApply = true, xpLogBox = true, xpLogScroll = true, lblXpLogTitle = true,
			                 xpHiRow = true, dynXpHiType = true, dynXpHiTrait = true, dynXpHiLevel = true, dynXpHiCost = true, dynXpHiWhen = true };

			-- The four columns take the SAME height, in one call: four heights written apart
			-- from each other scroll four different amounts inside the shared box, which is the
			-- misalignment the shared parent exists to stop (SPEC V261b/d). The box itself is not
			-- here on purpose - nothing writes its height any more (SPEC I57, V261a).
			--
			-- Declared local and ABOVE its caller, which is not a matter of reading order: Lua
			-- resolves a non-local name at COMPILE time, so a body written above the declaration
			-- compiles a GETGLOBAL and calls nil (SPEC B51, V223).
			local function xpColsHeight(form, h)
				form.dynXpType.height  = h;
				form.dynXpTrait.height = h;
				form.dynXpLevel.height = h;
				form.dynXpCost.height  = h;
				form.dynXpWhen.height  = h;
			end;

			-- 23rd batch (SPEC I167a, V486d, B166): the five columns carry no text any more, so
			-- what a state of the log takes down is the sixty ROWS. All sixty, unconditionally,
			-- and not the ones the caller happens to know about: a row left standing is a record
			-- on screen that the ledger no longer has - V473c coming through the text door, the
			-- same reason xpHiDrop runs above all three branches.
			local function xpRowsHide(form)
				for i = 1, XP_REV_POOL, 1 do
					local r = form["xpRow_" .. i];

					if r ~= nil then r.visible = false; end;
				end;
			end;

			-- The two empty states differ by their sentence and by NOTHING else, so they are
			-- written once. Two copies is how one of them keeps a stale height - which used to
			-- mean a box that had grown to forty rows stayed that tall and empty the moment the
			-- baseline came off, and now means a scroll range with nothing in it.
			local function xpEmptyLog(form, why)
				xpRowsHide(form);

				form.dynXpEmpty.text    = why;
				form.dynXpEmpty.visible = true;

				xpColsHeight(form, XP_LOG_MIN_H);
			end;

			-- The storyteller's revert buttons, one per row (SPEC I162d, V473, V474a). Lit only
			-- for the storyteller, only where there is a row, and never on a MANUAL or Ritual
			-- line - those the storyteller already owns directly (SPEC V474f, Q82.3). Every
			-- state of the log passes through here, so a button never outlives its row. The
			-- bound is the root's constant, the same one the gate measures the XML against
			-- (SPEC V383b); a row past it - the oldest, at the bottom - simply has no button.
			-- Where the template put the buttons, read ONCE and before anything writes over it
			-- (SPEC I163c). The number lives in the XML and nowhere else: repeating it here would
			-- be a second owner of a coordinate, which is what V478b exists to stop.
			local xpRevX = nil;

			local function xpRevButtons(form, rows, st)
				-- `st` arrives from renderXPLedger now (SPEC I163d, V474a as amended): the width of
				-- the two boxes asks the same question about the same person, and asking it twice
				-- per render is the second reading that leg refuses.
				if xpRevX == nil then xpRevX = form.btnXpRev_1.left; end;

				for i = 1, XP_REV_POOL, 1 do
					local r = (st and rows ~= nil) and rows[i] or nil;
					local b = form["btnXpRev_" .. i];

					b.visible = (r ~= nil and r.kind ~= "MANUAL" and r.kind ~= "Ritual" and r.kind ~= "Power");

					-- Out of the player's scroll RANGE, not merely out of sight (SPEC I163c,
					-- V478d): his box closes 30px earlier and a child sitting past that edge can
					-- hand the box a horizontal bar - gui.ScrollBox carries no property that would
					-- say otherwise (SPEC R104). Written inside the loop that was already here, so
					-- it costs no walk of its own.
					b.left = st and xpRevX or 0;
				end;
			end;

			-- The two boxes are narrower for a PLAYER, by exactly the column of X buttons he does
			-- not have (SPEC I163b, V478). The XML authors the PLAYER for the same reason the
			-- buttons are authored hidden (SPEC V473c): the sheet has to read right before any Lua
			-- runs, and a player is what most openings are.
			--
			-- Five widths and ONE number: the log box, the scroll inside it, the title over it,
			-- the APPLY box beside it and the button inside that one. They move together or the
			-- two bands stop closing on the same x (SPEC V247 as amended).
			--
			-- The authored widths are stamped on the first render rather than read back every time:
			-- adding to a width that has already been added to compounds, and the box would walk
			-- right across the tab one render at a time.
			local xpWidth0 = nil;

			local function xpLogWidth(form, st)
				if form.xpLogBox == nil then return; end;

				if xpWidth0 == nil then
					xpWidth0 = {
						box    = form.xpLogBox.width,
						scroll = form.xpLogScroll.width,
						title  = form.lblXpLogTitle.width,
						apply  = form.xpApplyBox.width,
						button = form.btnXpApply.width,
					};
				end;

				local add = st and XP_REV_W or 0;

				form.xpLogBox.width      = xpWidth0.box + add;
				form.xpLogScroll.width   = xpWidth0.scroll + add;
				form.lblXpLogTitle.width = xpWidth0.title + add;
				form.xpApplyBox.width    = xpWidth0.apply + add;
				form.btnXpApply.width    = xpWidth0.button + add;

				-- The ornament is drawn PER BOX, from the size the box had when it was painted
				-- (SPEC I72d, V284): the two boxes just changed size and the filigree would keep
				-- the shape it was born with, with nothing raised anywhere (SPEC B163). I72d used
				-- to say the health track was the only box that moves at runtime; the 19th batch
				-- made that false and no check noticed, which is why V482 counts these two.
				--
				-- The WIDTH is HANDED OVER rather than read back (SPEC V285c, B62): asking the box
				-- how wide it is on this side of the write can still answer with the role it is
				-- leaving, and the redraw then compares equal and skips. The HEIGHT is authored and
				-- never written, so reading it is safe - the exact inverse of renderHealthTrack,
				-- which hands the height it just wrote and reads the width it never touches.
				--
				-- Guarded because refreshOrnament is a GLOBAL living in WoD20.6's chunk, and that
				-- script may not have run when this one did (SPEC V480e, T1044).
				if refreshOrnament ~= nil then
					refreshOrnament(form.xpLogBox, xpWidth0.box + add, form.xpLogBox.height);
					refreshOrnament(form.xpApplyBox, xpWidth0.apply + add, form.xpApplyBox.height);
				end;
			end;

			-- The APPLY box (SPEC I162f, I162h, V475d/e): shown once the initial character is
			-- saved, and its button lives only while a purchase is still pending - a line whose
			-- key is not in the applied stamp. Dims in the same breath it locks (SPEC V112), at
			-- the user's own number (SPEC V476). Called from renderXPLedger and nowhere else:
			-- the log already redraws on every event that could change the answer.
			local function renderXPApply(form, rows)
				-- The box itself is never written any more (SPEC I163e, V479b): it stands where the
				-- XML put it and it is always on screen, so what speaks is the BUTTON. With no
				-- baseline there is nothing pending, and nothing pending is already the dimmed,
				-- disabled state this routine writes.
				local pending = false;

				if rows ~= nil then
					for i = 1, #rows, 1 do
						if string.find(sheet.xpApplied or "", "|" .. rows[i].key .. "|", 1, true) == nil then
							pending = true;
							break;
						end;
					end;
				end;

				form.btnXpApply.enabled = pending;
				form.btnXpApply.opacity = pending and 1 or 0.50;
			end;

			-- ---- The row highlight (SPEC I165, V484; user 2026-09-07) ----------------------
			--
			-- What the last render put on screen, kept so a hover can read row N without walking
			-- the ledger for it (SPEC I165e, V484e). renderXPLedger builds these five lists
			-- already, for the five table.concat below; holding on to them costs one assignment
			-- and saves a ledger walk PER BUTTON THE MOUSE CROSSES, which is the arithmetic that
			-- froze the sheet in B34 (SPEC V125).
			local xpHiKinds, xpHiTraits, xpHiLevels, xpHiCosts, xpHiWhens = nil, nil, nil, nil, nil;

			-- What the confirm dialog holds (SPEC I165d): the storyteller clicks the X, the modal
			-- takes the pointer off the button, and the leave that follows must NOT undo the
			-- highlight the click just fixed. Cleared in the first line of the answer, so OK and
			-- Cancel come out of it the same way.
			local xpHiArmed = false;

			-- The ONE place the band is taken down, and it is unconditional: renderXPLedger calls
			-- it above all three of its states (SPEC I165g), and xpHiHide/xpHiClear reach it after
			-- their own lookup. A highlight that outlives its render is a highlight sitting on
			-- somebody else's record - V473c coming through the text door.
			--
			-- Declared local and ABOVE its callers, which is not reading order: Lua resolves a
			-- non-local name at COMPILE time, so a body written above the declaration compiles a
			-- GETGLOBAL and calls nil (SPEC B51, V223).
			local function xpHiDrop(form)
				xpHiArmed = false;

				if form.xpHiRow ~= nil then form.xpHiRow.visible = false; end;
			end;

			-- Lit from the button's own onMouseEnter (SPEC I165c). GLOBAL because that is how the
			-- .lfm resolves a handler, exactly as onClick reaches xpRevert on the root.
			--
			-- Nothing here writes a font property: the band AUTHORS its size, weight and colour,
			-- and applyTheme paints the colour on the walk it already makes (SPEC I165f, B165).
			-- That is also why B21 cannot be reached from this routine - there is no font setter
			-- in it to detach anything from the form theme.
			function xpHiShow(node, i)
				-- The click outranks the pointer (SPEC I165d): while a revert waits on its answer,
				-- nothing the mouse does moves the band.
				if xpHiArmed or xpHiKinds == nil or xpHiKinds[i] == nil then return; end;

				local form = xpFind(node, XP_LOG, {});

				-- The tab is not on the sheet yet, or an older sheet has no band: nothing to draw
				-- on, and nothing to raise about it either (SPEC V56, V79).
				if form.xpHiRow == nil then return; end;

				form.dynXpHiType.text  = xpHiKinds[i];
				form.dynXpHiTrait.text = xpHiTraits[i];
				form.dynXpHiLevel.text = xpHiLevels[i];
				form.dynXpHiCost.text  = xpHiCosts[i];
				form.dynXpHiWhen.text  = xpHiWhens[i];

				-- The SAME step the columns and the sixty buttons are laid out on (SPEC V483c,
				-- V483d, V484d): row i of the band, row i of the text and row i of the X are one
				-- number, or they are three that drift apart sixty rows down.
				form.xpHiRow.top     = (i - 1) * XP_ROW_H;
				form.xpHiRow.visible = true;
			end;

			function xpHiHide(node)
				if xpHiArmed then return; end;

				xpHiDrop(xpFind(node, XP_LOG, {}));
			end;

			-- The two the ROOT calls (SPEC I165d, T1056): xpRevert arms one line before it asks
			-- and clears in the first line of the answer. Guarded there with `~= nil`, the way
			-- refreshOrnament and repaintXpDots are reached across chunks (SPEC V480e).
			function xpHiArm(node, i)
				xpHiShow(node, i);
				xpHiArmed = true;
			end;

			function xpHiClear(node)
				xpHiDrop(xpFind(node, XP_LOG, {}));
			end;

			function renderXPLedger(node, rows)
				if sheet == nil then return; end;

				-- The pool joins the roster on the first render and not at load (SPEC V473e): its
				-- count is the root's constant, and the root's script may not have run when this
				-- tab's did. Sixty names typed out here would drift from it (SPEC V383b).
				if XP_LOG.btnXpRev_1 == nil then
					for i = 1, XP_REV_POOL, 1 do
						-- EVERY control of row i, and that is not thoroughness - it is what the
						-- roster IS (SPEC B167, V486f). xpFind does not return the form: it walks
						-- the tree and returns a TABLE holding exactly the controls whose names
						-- are in here (WoD20th.lfm, `out[nm] = c`). A name left off is not a slow
						-- lookup, it is nil, and `form["dynXpType_1"].text = ...` is an index of
						-- nil the moment the first record is drawn.
						--
						-- All of them on ONE walk and off the constant (SPEC I167a, V473e): the
						-- pool bounds the buttons AND what the log can show, so a second loop
						-- here would be a second owner of one count (SPEC V383b).
						XP_LOG["btnXpRev_" .. i]   = true;
						XP_LOG["xpRow_" .. i]      = true;
						XP_LOG["dynXpType_" .. i]  = true;
						XP_LOG["dynXpTrait_" .. i] = true;
						XP_LOG["dynXpLevel_" .. i] = true;
						XP_LOG["dynXpCost_" .. i]  = true;
						XP_LOG["dynXpWhen_" .. i]  = true;
					end;
				end;

				local form = xpFind(node, XP_LOG, {});

				-- The tab is not on the sheet yet: nothing to draw on, and nothing to raise
				-- about it either.
				if form.dynXpType == nil then return; end;

				-- ABOVE all three states of the log, so every one of them takes the band down in
				-- the same line (SPEC I165g, V484): no baseline, nothing bought, and rows to read.
				-- The rows are about to be rebuilt, so a band still standing is a band standing on
				-- a record that may no longer be there.
				xpHiDrop(form);

				local lang = sheet.language or "pt";

				-- Read ONCE per render and handed to both routines that ask it (SPEC I163d, V474a
				-- as amended): whether the X column exists and whether the two boxes are 30px wider
				-- are the same question about the same person.
				local st = isStoryteller();

				-- Before the empty-log branch below, so the boxes are the shape of the ROLE in all
				-- three states - exactly what V235c already asks of the height (SPEC V478e).
				xpLogWidth(form, st);

				if rows == nil then rows = xpLedgerRows(); end;

				-- SPEC V33 and V98: say WHY it is empty rather than show four blank columns. The two
				-- states differ in the sentence and in nothing else, and neither of them moves a
				-- column: an empty log has the shape of a full one (SPEC V235).
				if rows == nil or #rows == 0 then
					local why = "Nothing bought yet";

					if rows == nil then why = "Initial character not saved yet"; end;

					xpEmptyLog(form, translateSheetText(why, lang));

					-- An empty log has no record to highlight, and the cache says so (SPEC I165e):
					-- a hover arriving on a button that has not been taken down yet finds nothing
					-- to read and draws nothing, rather than the row that used to be there.
					xpHiKinds, xpHiTraits, xpHiLevels, xpHiCosts, xpHiWhens = nil, nil, nil, nil, nil;

					-- The two side controls follow every state of the log (SPEC V474f, V475e):
					-- no rows, no buttons, and the APPLY box only once a baseline exists.
					xpRevButtons(form, nil, st);
					renderXPApply(form, rows);
					return;
				end;

				local kinds, traits, levels, costs, whens = {}, {}, {}, {}, {};

				for i = 1, #rows, 1 do
					local r = rows[i];

					-- Both columns are written by Lua, so they are translated here (SPEC V70,
					-- same arrangement as the era ability labels): whoever writes a text owns
					-- both languages for it.
					kinds[i]  = translateSheetText(r.kind, lang);
					traits[i] = translateSheetText(r.name, lang);

					if r.gone then traits[i] = traits[i] .. " " .. translateSheetText("(removed, no refund)", lang); end;
					levels[i] = tostring(r.level);

					-- Just the figure, in every row (SPEC V120): the log prints what the point
					-- cost and nothing else - no word to keep in two languages.
					costs[i] = tostring(r.cost);

					-- The hour, in the reader's own order (SPEC I162c, Q82.2): stored ISO so it
					-- sorts and needs no key, shown day-first in Portuguese. A line with no stamp
					-- - bought before the 18th batch - reads a dash, not an invented hour.
					if r.when == nil then
						whens[i] = "-";
					elseif lang == "pt" then
						whens[i] = (string.gsub(r.when, "^(%d+)-(%d+)-(%d+)", "%3/%2/%1"));
					else
						whens[i] = r.when;
					end;
				end;

				-- Rows to read: the sentence steps aside and the columns stay exactly where they
				-- are. Nothing here writes a width (SPEC V235a).
				form.dynXpEmpty.visible = false;

				-- ONE ROW, ONE CONTROL (SPEC I167a, V486d, B166). The five columns used to be five
				-- text blocks joined with a blank line between records, and where line i LANDED
				-- was the HOST's answer: the rendered line height is not a number this SDK will
				-- tell anyone (SPEC R165b). XP_LINE_H = 16 was never measured, the real pair came
				-- out near 27, and the X drifted about 5px further from its record every row -
				-- by the thirteenth it was pointing at a different purchase. A row is a layout of
				-- XP_ROW_H now, so row i is where the arithmetic says it is and nowhere else.
				--
				-- The pool is also the CEILING of what the log can show (SPEC I167d, Q87): the SDK
				-- builds no control at runtime (SPEC R162), and xpLedgerRows() hands the newest
				-- first, so past XP_REV_POOL what falls off the end is the OLDEST - and it had no
				-- revert button to lose either (SPEC V473b).
				for i = 1, XP_REV_POOL, 1 do
					local row = form["xpRow_" .. i];

					if row ~= nil then
						if kinds[i] ~= nil then
							form["dynXpType_"  .. i].text = kinds[i];
							form["dynXpTrait_" .. i].text = traits[i];
							form["dynXpLevel_" .. i].text = levels[i];
							form["dynXpCost_"  .. i].text = costs[i];
							form["dynXpWhen_"  .. i].text = whens[i];

							row.visible = true;
						else
							row.visible = false;
						end;
					end;
				end;

				-- The SAME five lists the columns were just written from, kept for the highlight
				-- (SPEC I165e, V484e). Written here rather than rebuilt on hover: what the band
				-- shows for row i has to be what row i of the columns says, and two places that
				-- format the same row are two that can disagree about it (SPEC V125).
				xpHiKinds, xpHiTraits, xpHiLevels, xpHiCosts, xpHiWhens = kinds, traits, levels, costs, whens;

				-- The columns grow, the box does not (SPEC I57). Past the floor this runs longer
				-- than the scrollBox holding it, which is what puts a bar on the side instead of
				-- pushing the frame down the tab. The five columns are GROUND now (SPEC I167c) and
				-- carry no text, but they are still what the scroll measures its reach by: a
				-- gui.ScrollBox has no range property of its own, so the children own it (R104).
				--
				-- The reach is what the pool can SHOW and not what the ledger holds (SPEC I167d):
				-- a 61st record has no row to scroll down to.
				local shown = #rows;

				if shown > XP_REV_POOL then shown = XP_REV_POOL; end;

				local h = XP_LOG_MIN_H;

				if shown * XP_ROW_H > h then h = shown * XP_ROW_H; end;

				xpColsHeight(form, h);

				-- The two side controls follow every state of the log (SPEC V474f, V475d).
				xpRevButtons(form, rows, st);
				renderXPApply(form, rows);
			end;

			-- Apply Spent Experience (SPEC I162g, V475a; user 2026-09-07). Every line the log
			-- shows becomes a floor the player cannot sell under (SPEC V103 as amended); the
			-- stamp is REPLACED, not appended to, so a key is never carried twice. Asks first,
			-- the way saveBaseline does (SPEC V81, R161), and writes only inside the answer.
			-- One walk per click (SPEC V125): the rows priced before the question are the rows
			-- applied after it.
			function xpApply(form)
				if sheet == nil then return; end;

				local rows = xpLedgerRows();

				if rows == nil then return; end;

				local lang = sheet.language or "pt";

				Dialogs.confirmOkCancel(
					translateSheetText("Apply the spent experience? The points bought stay on the sheet and can no longer be removed.", lang),
					function(ok)
						if not ok or sheet == nil then return; end;

						local keys = {};

						for i = 1, #rows, 1 do keys[i] = "|" .. rows[i].key .. "|"; end;

						setField("xpApplied", table.concat(keys));

						-- The dots stop being pending in the same breath (SPEC I163h, V480e). It
						-- is a call of its own and not a side effect of the line below: the dots
						-- live on the trait tabs, and renderXPLedger only ever draws this one.
						-- The rows priced before the question are the rows repainted after it
						-- (SPEC V125).
						if repaintXpDots ~= nil then repaintXpDots(form, rows); end;

						-- The whole log again rather than the box alone: one path finds the
						-- controls (SPEC V143), and the log is where the answer is read from.
						renderXPLedger(form, rows);
					end);
			end;
		]]>
	</script>
	<scrollBox align="client">
		<!-- Theme backdrop, same arrangement as WoD20.1 (SPEC T158, V56, V57). -->
		<image name="themePaper9" align="client" style="stretch" hitTest="false" visible="false"/>

		<!-- One revert button per ledger row, for the storyteller (SPEC I162d, V473, R162). A
		     pool authored here rather than built at runtime: the SDK has no gui.newButton, and
		     building a form per row is the probe T496 never ran. Born hidden - the XML has to
		     read right for a PLAYER before any Lua runs - and lit by renderXPLedger after
		     isStoryteller(). `top` is (num - 1) * XP_ROW_H + PAD, so button N sits on row N of
		     the columns beside it; `X` is the glyph the ? box already closes with (SPEC Q82.5).

		     PAD is the SAME 8 the five columns open at (SPEC I166b, V485c). That is the whole
		     of "the button's centre is aligned with the text's centre": the button and the line
		     it reverts are two controls of XP_LINE_H at the same `top`, and two controls of one
		     height at one top share a centre. Said as a RELATION the gate measures on both
		     sides, never as a centre somebody computed here (SPEC V383b) - which is also what
		     keeps it true if PAD is ever amended.

		     The button is also what HIGHLIGHTS the record it would revert (SPEC I165c, V484,
		     user 2026-09-07): the pointer lands on the X and the whole row - all five columns -
		     comes up bigger, bold and in the era's accent. The events are the SDK's own, declared
		     on gui.Control and bound by name exactly as onClick is (SPEC R165a), so a button
		     inside a template takes them the same way. Record 61 and past it carry no button, so
		     they carry no highlight either: the highlight follows the BUTTON, not the row.

		     23rd batch (SPEC I167a, V486, B166): the record itself is a LAYOUT now, one per row,
		     and the five labels inside it are vertTextAlign="center". The columns used to be five
		     tall textEditors carrying every record as text joined with a blank line, and where
		     record i landed was then the HOST's answer - the rendered line height, which this SDK
		     reports to nobody (SPEC R165b). The step said 32, the screen said about 27, and the X
		     walked ~5px further from its record every row. Now the row is 32 because the XML says
		     32, the label is centred in it and the button is centred beside it, so record 1 and
		     record 60 line up for the same reason and nothing accumulates.

		     The button stays a SIBLING of the row rather than a child of it, and that is the one
		     thing here that is not tidiness: a layout wide enough to hold it would be 765 inside
		     the player's 751-wide scroll, and gui.ScrollBox has no property that would refuse the
		     horizontal bar that follows (SPEC R104, V478d). So the row closes on the last column
		     at 735 and the button is placed by the same arithmetic from outside it. -->
		<template name="XpLogRow">
			<layout name="xpRow_$(num)" left="0" top="$(top)" width="735" height="32" visible="false">
				<label name="dynXpType_$(num)" left="15" top="0" width="133" height="32" vertTextAlign="center" fontSize="12" opacity="0.75"/>
				<label name="dynXpTrait_$(num)" left="158" top="0" width="200" height="32" vertTextAlign="center" fontSize="12" opacity="0.75"/>
				<label name="dynXpLevel_$(num)" left="368" top="0" width="82" height="32" vertTextAlign="center" horzTextAlign="center" fontSize="12" opacity="0.75"/>
				<label name="dynXpCost_$(num)" left="460" top="0" width="45" height="32" vertTextAlign="center" horzTextAlign="center" fontSize="12" opacity="0.75"/>
				<label name="dynXpWhen_$(num)" left="515" top="0" width="220" height="32" vertTextAlign="center" horzTextAlign="center" fontSize="12" opacity="0.75"/>
			</layout>
		</template>
		<template name="XpRevRow">
			<button name="btnXpRev_$(num)" left="745" top="$(top)" width="20" height="16" text="X" visible="false" onClick="xpRevert(self, $(num));" onMouseEnter="xpHiShow(self, $(num));" onMouseLeave="xpHiHide(self);"/>
		</template>
		<!--
			Progress tab (SPEC T106). Current Experience used to be an NDB mirror of the box on
			the Main tab, both owning `field="experience"`. Neither owns a field now (SPEC I11):
			both are written by renderXPBoxes and both hand what the player types to
			xpSetCurrent, so they stay in step through the one number that IS saved.
		-->
		<!--
			The three numbers sit side by side (SPEC T140): one column each, same label width and
			same edit column, so the three edits line up (SPEC V26). The box spans the full tab
			width, which is what the log box below is aligned to.
		-->
		<!--
			Three columns of 336, each holding its label with the number CENTRED UNDER IT rather than
			beside it (SPEC T151). The title sits 10 off the top edge and the data starts 8 under it:
			room above the heading, none wasted between the heading and what it names.

			Stacking costs height - the box went 75 -> 95 - which is why the log below starts at 105
			and still closes the tab on 640.
		-->
		<layout left="0" top="0" width="561" height="120">
			<rectangle align="client" color="black" xradius="14" yradius="14" cornerType="innerRound"/>
			<label left="20" top="15" width="521" height="20" text="EXPERIENCE" horzTextAlign="center"/>

			<label left="36" top="50" width="141" height="20" text="Total Experience" horzTextAlign="center"/>
			<!-- All SIX experience numbers stand on a darker ground than the box (SPEC V256 as
			     amended, V417, user 2026-09-04). It takes THREE things and any one of them alone
			     does nothing: the rectangle declared BEFORE the edit so it paints behind it,
			     transparent="true" on the edit so its own ground stops covering it, and a colour
			     with a key in all four palettes so the theme owns the shade (SPEC V53). gui.Edit has
			     no background colour of its own - MEASURED in the SDK, its props are textPrompt/
			     transparent/field/frameRegion/type/min/max/decimalPlaces/asNumber - so the rectangle
			     is the only way to say this.

			     Current used to be left OUT of this, on the reading that a dark ground was a LOCK
			     cue. The owner asked for the same border on every Experience field, so the ground
			     is a FIELD frame now - "you type the number here" - and the lock cue is opacity
			     alone (SPEC V111a): the two locked ones keep 0.75 and the four editable ones carry
			     no opacity at all, which is what V111(b) already asked for. -->
			<rectangle name="bgTotalXP" left="61" top="80" width="90" height="25" color="#0A0A0A" xradius="6" yradius="6" cornerType="innerRound" hitTest="false"/>
			<edit name="edtTotalXP" type="number" decimalPlaces="0" left="61" top="80" width="90" height="25" readOnly="true" opacity="0.75" horzTextAlign="center" vertTextAlign="center" fontSize="14" transparent="true"/>

			<label left="210" top="50" width="141" height="20" text="Spent Experience" horzTextAlign="center"/>
			<!-- Authored LOCKED, and the lock moves at runtime with the storyteller's flag
			     (SPEC I44, V245): the sheet has to read right before any Lua runs, and OFF is
			     what the flag defaults to. Typing here writes a MANUAL line into the log, never
			     a second number beside it (SPEC V246). -->
			<rectangle name="bgSpentXP" left="235" top="80" width="90" height="25" color="#0A0A0A" xradius="6" yradius="6" cornerType="innerRound" hitTest="false"/>
			<edit name="edtSpentXP" type="number" decimalPlaces="0" left="235" top="80" width="90" height="25" readOnly="true" opacity="0.75" horzTextAlign="center" vertTextAlign="center" fontSize="14" transparent="true" onUserChange="xpSetSpent(self, self.edtSpentXP.text);"/>

			<label left="384" top="50" width="141" height="20" text="Current Experience" horzTextAlign="center"/>
			<rectangle name="bgCurrentXP" left="409" top="80" width="90" height="25" color="#0A0A0A" xradius="6" yradius="6" cornerType="innerRound" hitTest="false"/>
			<edit name="edtCurrentXP" type="number" decimalPlaces="0" left="409" top="80" width="90" height="25" horzTextAlign="center" vertTextAlign="center" fontSize="14" onUserChange="xpSetCurrent(self, self.edtCurrentXP.text);" transparent="true"/>
		</layout>

		<!--
			The Apply box (SPEC I162f as amended, I163e, user 2026-09-07): to the right of
			EXPERIENCE with the 5px gap every pair of section boxes keeps (SPEC V298, I76a - the
			20 of V280b was revoked, SPEC B160), and closing on the same x the log box below
			closes on (SPEC V247 as amended).

			No TITLE, on the owner's word (SPEC I163e): one button needs no heading over it. That
			takes it out of the definition of a section box, which is a layout with a rectangle
			AND a centred title (SPEC V48), so it is a declared exception there instead - by
			construction, exactly as the tab strip's floor is (SPEC V68, I163f). Its fill and its
			corner stay identical to its sisters': what changed is which check owns it.

			ALWAYS visible (SPEC I163e, V479b). Nothing writes the box any more - what speaks is
			the button, lit only while a purchase is still unapplied (SPEC V475e), dimmed at 0.50,
			the user's own number and the one place on the sheet it is allowed (SPEC V476). It is
			authored disabled all the same: most openings have no baseline, and the sheet has to
			read right before any Lua runs.

			The width is the PLAYER'S (SPEC I163b, V478a): 791 - 561 - 5. The storyteller's log is
			30 wider for his column of X buttons, and xpLogWidth grows this box and the button
			inside it by the same 30, so the two bands close on one x for both readers.
		-->
		<layout name="xpApplyBox" left="566" top="0" width="225" height="120">
			<rectangle align="client" color="black" xradius="14" yradius="14" cornerType="innerRound"/>
			<!-- Centred in the box now that no title sits over it: (120 - 26) / 2 (SPEC I163e,
			     V479d). The 20 either side is the margin every box keeps (SPEC V280a). -->
			<button name="btnXpApply" left="20" top="47" width="185" height="26" text="Apply Spent Experience" enabled="false" opacity="0.50" onClick="xpApply(self);"/>
		</layout>

		<!--
			The log is now four columns instead of one free-text box (SPEC I10, 27th round):
			what was raised, which trait, the rating it reached, and what that point cost.
			`xpLog` is gone with the box that owned it and is an ORPHAN from here on (SPEC I3 /
			V91) - the name is never reused, so text typed into the old sheet cannot come back
			up inside a column that means something else.

			The four editors are read-only and carry no `field`: the rows are derived on every
			render (SPEC V83/V84).

			The box is a FIXED height from the 90th round on (SPEC I57): the title and the four
			column headings belong to it, the rows belong to the scrollBox below them, and
			nothing in Lua writes this height any more.
		-->
		<layout name="xpLogBox" left="0" top="125" width="791" height="550">
			<rectangle align="client" color="black" xradius="14" yradius="14" cornerType="innerRound"/>
			<!-- The title needs a NAME from the 19th batch on (SPEC I163b, V478a): it is one of the
			     five widths that follow the reader's role, or it would sit 15px off centre in the
			     storyteller's wider box. Its width is the scroll's, in both states. -->
			<label name="lblXpLogTitle" left="20" top="15" width="751" height="20" text="EXPERIENCE LOG" horzTextAlign="center"/>

			<!--
				Widths asked for by the user on top of the measured ones (33rd round): Type -30%,
				Trait -20%, Level -10%, Cost -50%. The two text columns read from the left, the two
				short ones are CENTRED - a rating and a price are read as a column of figures, not
				as a sentence.

				What the measurements say has to fit, at the widest of the four theme fonts (Georgia
				and Book Antiqua run wider than Segoe UI at the same size): "FORÇA DE VONTADE" 134px
				at a 12px body and 157px at 14px, "Empatia com Animais" 126/147px, "Grátis" 38/44px.
				Type and Cost are now UNDER their 14px figure - the trim was asked for with the
				sheet on screen, so what renders is the judge; if a kind name or the word Grátis
				comes up cut in the Dark Ages or Classical paper, these two are what to widen.

				85th round: Level went 54 -> 82 to hold the word MANUAL, the one row whose Level
				is a word and not a rating (SPEC I44, V246). Cost moved with it and both boxes
				grew by the same 26, so the four columns keep their gaps and the two boxes still
				close on the same x (SPEC V247) - a column that grows while its box stands still
				is how the log would have started poking out of its own frame.
			-->
			<label left="35" top="40" width="133" height="20" text="Type" horzTextAlign="center"/>
			<label left="178" top="40" width="200" height="20" text="Trait" horzTextAlign="center"/>
			<label left="388" top="40" width="82" height="20" text="Level" horzTextAlign="center"/>
			<label left="480" top="40" width="45" height="20" text="Cost" horzTextAlign="center"/>
			<!-- The fifth column's heading, over dynXpWhen (515 inside the scroll, 535 here), and
			     OUTSIDE the scrollBox like the four beside it (SPEC I162c, V261c). -->
			<label left="535" top="40" width="220" height="20" text="Date/Time" horzTextAlign="center"/>

			<!--
				The four columns scroll TOGETHER, inside one box of their own (SPEC I57, user
				2026-08-23). Giving each column a bar would let them scroll four different
				amounts and line N would stop reading across the row - which is exactly why the
				log used to grow the frame instead of scrolling it.

				It sits at left=20 top=70 since I73 gave every box a 20px margin, so a column's x is
				read INSIDE the scrolling box and not against the frame: V247 measures the Cost
				column against this box, and this box against the frame (SPEC I73, V247).

				The 16 to the right of the Cost column is the room the vertical bar has to come
				out of. gui.ScrollBox carries no properties of its own - no axis, no bar width,
				nothing to hide the horizontal one - so whether 16 is enough is a question for
				the screen, not for the XML (SPEC R104).
			-->
			<scrollBox name="xpLogScroll" left="20" top="65" width="751" height="470">
				<textEditor name="dynXpType" left="15" top="0" width="133" height="470" readOnly="true" opacity="0.75" fontSize="12"/>
				<textEditor name="dynXpTrait" left="158" top="0" width="200" height="470" readOnly="true" opacity="0.75" fontSize="12"/>
				<textEditor name="dynXpLevel" left="368" top="0" width="82" height="470" readOnly="true" opacity="0.75" fontSize="12" horzTextAlign="center"/>
				<textEditor name="dynXpCost" left="460" top="0" width="45" height="470" readOnly="true" opacity="0.75" fontSize="12" horzTextAlign="center"/>
				<!-- The hour of the purchase (SPEC I162c, V472e): centred like the two figures, and
				     220 wide so this band and the one above close on the same x (SPEC V247). -->
				<textEditor name="dynXpWhen" left="515" top="0" width="220" height="470" readOnly="true" opacity="0.75" fontSize="12" horzTextAlign="center"/>
				<!-- The storyteller's revert buttons, XP_REV_POOL of them, one per row (SPEC I162d,
				     V473b): the rightmost children of the scroll, and what the 16px bar reserve
				     and the box width are measured from (SPEC V247). -->
				<XpLogRow num="1" top="0"/>
				<XpLogRow num="2" top="32"/>
				<XpLogRow num="3" top="64"/>
				<XpLogRow num="4" top="96"/>
				<XpLogRow num="5" top="128"/>
				<XpLogRow num="6" top="160"/>
				<XpLogRow num="7" top="192"/>
				<XpLogRow num="8" top="224"/>
				<XpLogRow num="9" top="256"/>
				<XpLogRow num="10" top="288"/>
				<XpLogRow num="11" top="320"/>
				<XpLogRow num="12" top="352"/>
				<XpLogRow num="13" top="384"/>
				<XpLogRow num="14" top="416"/>
				<XpLogRow num="15" top="448"/>
				<XpLogRow num="16" top="480"/>
				<XpLogRow num="17" top="512"/>
				<XpLogRow num="18" top="544"/>
				<XpLogRow num="19" top="576"/>
				<XpLogRow num="20" top="608"/>
				<XpLogRow num="21" top="640"/>
				<XpLogRow num="22" top="672"/>
				<XpLogRow num="23" top="704"/>
				<XpLogRow num="24" top="736"/>
				<XpLogRow num="25" top="768"/>
				<XpLogRow num="26" top="800"/>
				<XpLogRow num="27" top="832"/>
				<XpLogRow num="28" top="864"/>
				<XpLogRow num="29" top="896"/>
				<XpLogRow num="30" top="928"/>
				<XpLogRow num="31" top="960"/>
				<XpLogRow num="32" top="992"/>
				<XpLogRow num="33" top="1024"/>
				<XpLogRow num="34" top="1056"/>
				<XpLogRow num="35" top="1088"/>
				<XpLogRow num="36" top="1120"/>
				<XpLogRow num="37" top="1152"/>
				<XpLogRow num="38" top="1184"/>
				<XpLogRow num="39" top="1216"/>
				<XpLogRow num="40" top="1248"/>
				<XpLogRow num="41" top="1280"/>
				<XpLogRow num="42" top="1312"/>
				<XpLogRow num="43" top="1344"/>
				<XpLogRow num="44" top="1376"/>
				<XpLogRow num="45" top="1408"/>
				<XpLogRow num="46" top="1440"/>
				<XpLogRow num="47" top="1472"/>
				<XpLogRow num="48" top="1504"/>
				<XpLogRow num="49" top="1536"/>
				<XpLogRow num="50" top="1568"/>
				<XpLogRow num="51" top="1600"/>
				<XpLogRow num="52" top="1632"/>
				<XpLogRow num="53" top="1664"/>
				<XpLogRow num="54" top="1696"/>
				<XpLogRow num="55" top="1728"/>
				<XpLogRow num="56" top="1760"/>
				<XpLogRow num="57" top="1792"/>
				<XpLogRow num="58" top="1824"/>
				<XpLogRow num="59" top="1856"/>
				<XpLogRow num="60" top="1888"/>

				<XpRevRow num="1" top="8"/>
				<XpRevRow num="2" top="40"/>
				<XpRevRow num="3" top="72"/>
				<XpRevRow num="4" top="104"/>
				<XpRevRow num="5" top="136"/>
				<XpRevRow num="6" top="168"/>
				<XpRevRow num="7" top="200"/>
				<XpRevRow num="8" top="232"/>
				<XpRevRow num="9" top="264"/>
				<XpRevRow num="10" top="296"/>
				<XpRevRow num="11" top="328"/>
				<XpRevRow num="12" top="360"/>
				<XpRevRow num="13" top="392"/>
				<XpRevRow num="14" top="424"/>
				<XpRevRow num="15" top="456"/>
				<XpRevRow num="16" top="488"/>
				<XpRevRow num="17" top="520"/>
				<XpRevRow num="18" top="552"/>
				<XpRevRow num="19" top="584"/>
				<XpRevRow num="20" top="616"/>
				<XpRevRow num="21" top="648"/>
				<XpRevRow num="22" top="680"/>
				<XpRevRow num="23" top="712"/>
				<XpRevRow num="24" top="744"/>
				<XpRevRow num="25" top="776"/>
				<XpRevRow num="26" top="808"/>
				<XpRevRow num="27" top="840"/>
				<XpRevRow num="28" top="872"/>
				<XpRevRow num="29" top="904"/>
				<XpRevRow num="30" top="936"/>
				<XpRevRow num="31" top="968"/>
				<XpRevRow num="32" top="1000"/>
				<XpRevRow num="33" top="1032"/>
				<XpRevRow num="34" top="1064"/>
				<XpRevRow num="35" top="1096"/>
				<XpRevRow num="36" top="1128"/>
				<XpRevRow num="37" top="1160"/>
				<XpRevRow num="38" top="1192"/>
				<XpRevRow num="39" top="1224"/>
				<XpRevRow num="40" top="1256"/>
				<XpRevRow num="41" top="1288"/>
				<XpRevRow num="42" top="1320"/>
				<XpRevRow num="43" top="1352"/>
				<XpRevRow num="44" top="1384"/>
				<XpRevRow num="45" top="1416"/>
				<XpRevRow num="46" top="1448"/>
				<XpRevRow num="47" top="1480"/>
				<XpRevRow num="48" top="1512"/>
				<XpRevRow num="49" top="1544"/>
				<XpRevRow num="50" top="1576"/>
				<XpRevRow num="51" top="1608"/>
				<XpRevRow num="52" top="1640"/>
				<XpRevRow num="53" top="1672"/>
				<XpRevRow num="54" top="1704"/>
				<XpRevRow num="55" top="1736"/>
				<XpRevRow num="56" top="1768"/>
				<XpRevRow num="57" top="1800"/>
				<XpRevRow num="58" top="1832"/>
				<XpRevRow num="59" top="1864"/>
				<XpRevRow num="60" top="1896"/>

				<!--
					The row highlight (SPEC I165a, V484): ONE band that moves, not sixty rows of
					controls. Font properties in this SDK belong to the whole control and
					gui.TextEditor carries no rich text, so a single line inside a five-column
					block cannot be styled on its own (SPEC R165b) - the band is drawn OVER the
					line instead. Sixty rows of labels was the other way out and I164c already
					refused it: it would cap the log at sixty records, and the columns show every
					record - only the X buttons stop at XP_REV_POOL.

					DECLARED LAST inside the scroll because the host paints in document order,
					which is the same reason dynXpEmpty sits where it does.

					The rectangle is what makes the band possible at all: the 12px line is still
					THERE, underneath, and would read through the bold 14px one. `color="black"`
					is the key xpLogBox itself authors, so the palette paints the two the same
					shade in all four eras (SPEC V53) and the band leaves no seam. No xradius, so
					sectionBox says no and the band takes neither filigree nor bevel (SPEC I89b,
					V316d).

					The five labels ARE the five columns, shifted by the layout's own 15: 0/143/
					353/445/500 against 15/158/368/460/515, at the same widths and the same
					alignment. It is a RELATION the gate measures on both sides (SPEC V484b) and
					not a second owner of a coordinate (SPEC V383b, V478b).

					fontColor is AUTHORED and that is the whole colour story (SPEC I165f, B165):
					`#C2A14D` is the era's TEXT accent - the key the open sub-tab's label already
					wears (SPEC I78c, V305d) - so applyTheme paints it on the walk it already
					makes and no Lua ever writes a font property here. The first draft published
					the STROKE accent instead, which is `#3A4150` over a `#12141A` ground in
					Modern Nights: a highlight darker than the text it highlights.

					No fontFamily, for V483b's reason one floor up: the era's serif does not reach
					the log, so the highlight changes SIZE, WEIGHT and COLOUR - never the face.

					The height is XP_ROW_H and the top is (i - 1) * XP_ROW_H, both unchanged since
					the 21st batch: the band IS the box the record sits in (SPEC I166d, V484d).

					vertTextAlign is CENTRE, and that is the 22nd batch (SPEC I166d, V485e). It
					used to be leading, on the reading that the 12px line underneath started at
					the top of the band - which it did, back when the columns opened at 0. They
					open at PAD now and the line falls in the MIDDLE of the box, so leading would
					stand the 14px text 8px above the 12px text it is covering, on every row. Two
					bodies centred in one box of 32 share that centre by construction, so the
					question T1058(a) was going to put to the screen dies in the arithmetic.

					The rectangle is also the BOX MADE VISIBLE (SPEC I166e, V485f, user
					2026-09-08). Outside the hover there is nothing to hide: it lives inside
					xpHiRow, which is authored visible="false", so "an invisible box, with no
					outline and no ornament" is the state the XML already ships. Under the
					pointer it is a 2px line in the colour the era draws its box ORNAMENTS with -
					#A83232, #D8BC72, #C0C6CC, #BFA05A (SPEC R166e, Q86.1).

					strokeColor="black" here is NOT that colour. It is the WITNESS that the XML
					authored the property at all, without which paint() returns early and never
					writes - the guard B21 left behind (SPEC R166b) - and it is what the box
					reads as before any theme has run. The colour that lands is t.ornament, and
					it lands from applyTheme because t.ornament is a VALUE and not a key: there
					is no strokeColor a palette could map to it (SPEC I166e).

					strokeSize="2" survives for one reason only: applyTheme writes THEME_STROKE=3
					over EVERY rectangle on the sheet, and WoD20.6 fences this one name out of
					that write (SPEC V485f, Q86.2). Without the fence the XML says 2, the screen
					says 3, rdk -l exits 0 and the gate stays green - B58 and B62 on a fifth axis.
				-->
				<layout name="xpHiRow" left="15" top="0" width="720" height="32" visible="false">
					<rectangle name="xpHiEdge" align="client" color="black" strokeColor="black" strokeSize="2"/>
					<label name="dynXpHiType" left="0" top="0" width="133" height="32" fontSize="14" fontStyle="bold" fontColor="#C2A14D" opacity="1" vertTextAlign="center"/>
					<label name="dynXpHiTrait" left="143" top="0" width="200" height="32" fontSize="14" fontStyle="bold" fontColor="#C2A14D" opacity="1" vertTextAlign="center"/>
					<label name="dynXpHiLevel" left="353" top="0" width="82" height="32" fontSize="14" fontStyle="bold" fontColor="#C2A14D" opacity="1" vertTextAlign="center" horzTextAlign="center"/>
					<label name="dynXpHiCost" left="445" top="0" width="45" height="32" fontSize="14" fontStyle="bold" fontColor="#C2A14D" opacity="1" vertTextAlign="center" horzTextAlign="center"/>
					<label name="dynXpHiWhen" left="500" top="0" width="220" height="32" fontSize="14" fontStyle="bold" fontColor="#C2A14D" opacity="1" vertTextAlign="center" horzTextAlign="center"/>
				</layout>
			</scrollBox>

			<!-- Why the log is empty, on a line of its own (SPEC I39, V235). Declared LAST because
			     it sits over the scrolling columns and the host paints in document order - the
			     same reason the Type column used to be last, now carried by the control that
			     actually needs it. Authored hidden: a log with rows never shows it, and the dyn*
			     prefix keeps the language traversal off it (SPEC V31).

			     OUTSIDE the scrollBox, and that is the point: it is the state of the BLOCK, not a
			     row in it, so it must not scroll away from the only state it is ever shown in
			     (SPEC V261c).

			     19th batch (SPEC I163a, V477, user 2026-09-07): it reads as a TRAIT now instead of
			     as a banner across the block. left is where the Trait column's text begins - the
			     scroll's own left plus that column's - and the width closes on the right edge of
			     the Cost column, which is why it needs no runtime write and does not grow with the
			     storyteller's log (SPEC V477b, V478c). Leading-aligned for the same reason: the
			     sentence starts where a trait name would start. The longest of the two, in pt,
			     wants about 215px of the 347. -->
			<label name="dynXpEmpty" left="178" top="65" width="347" height="20" text="" horzTextAlign="leading" opacity="0.75" visible="false"/>

		</layout>
	</scrollBox>

	<!-- Two entry points, as everywhere else on this sheet: the link catches later edits, the
	     onNodeReady catches the sheet being opened (SPEC V19 / V33). -->
	<dataLink field="xpTotal" onChange="renderXPBoxes(self);"/>

	<!-- Every one of these four moves the prices, so the three numbers are rebuilt with the log:
	     free-buy and the backgrounds rule change what a point costs, the snapshot changes what
	     counts as bought at all, and the language changes the two text columns.
	     What the ledger reads directly: the snapshot itself, the rule that prices backgrounds,
	     and the language its two text columns are written in. This tab watches `language`
	     itself rather than being called from WoD20.6, because the four columns are declared here
	     and only this form can address them. The dots are NOT watched from here - there are
	     some four hundred and sixty of them and no list of that size stays correct. What
	     redraws the log when one of them is clicked is the click itself: xpClick already runs
	     on every dot that costs experience and already holds the rows (SPEC V121/V125). -->
	<dataLink fields="{'baseline', 'stBackgroundCost', 'language'}" onChange="renderXPLedger(self); renderXPBoxes(self);"/>

	<!-- The storyteller's correction and the flag that allows it (SPEC I44, V245/V246). Both
	     are owned on WoD20.10 and watched from here, so a tick or a figure entered by ANOTHER
	     client at the table reaches this tab too - the same reason the clan lock hangs off a
	     dataLink rather than off the checkbox that writes it (SPEC V175, V95).

	     `xpManual` moves a LINE of the log and the Spent box with it; `stEditSpentXP` moves
	     only the lock, but it is painted by the same routine that writes the number, so the
	     two cannot come apart (SPEC V112). -->
	<dataLink fields="{'xpManual', 'stEditSpentXP'}" onChange="renderXPLedger(self); renderXPBoxes(self);"/>

	<!-- The tab is a REPORT, so it is rebuilt every time it comes up (SPEC V97). -->
	<event name="onShow">
		wodTrace("WoD20.9.onShow");
		renderXPBoxes(self);
		renderXPLedger(self);
	</event>

	<event name="onNodeReady">
		renderXPBoxes(self);
		renderXPLedger(self);

		-- Nothing is handed over any more (SPEC V133, 44th round). This tab used to register
		-- itself twice - once for the log and once for the three numbers - and a purchase
		-- reached neither until it had (SPEC B39). xpClick now walks from the sheet root and
		-- finds both by name, so a tab that was never opened costs nothing and a tab that is
		-- open is drawn whether or not it ever announced itself.
		--
		-- What DID have to leave for good is the whole-node ndb.newObserver that sat here
		-- until the 39th round (SPEC V121): it fired on EVERY attribute written - including
		-- the hundreds the sheet writes to itself while loading - and it did not pass through
		-- xpQuiet, so the shield that fixed B30 never covered it. On a sheet with a saved
		-- baseline that was a full walk of the ledger per write, and the sheet froze on open
		-- (SPEC B34).
	</event>
</form>
